@page "/future-plans"

<PageTitle>Future Plans</PageTitle>

<div class="container py-4" style="max-width: 720px;">
    <h1 class="mb-4">Future Plans</h1>

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4 border-success">
        <div class="card-body">
            <h4 class="card-title text-success">Phase 2 — MCP Tool Server</h4>
            <p class="text-muted mb-3">Model Context Protocol &middot; Verified spike &middot; Migration deferred</p>

            <div class="alert alert-success py-2 px-3 mb-3">
                <strong>Status (August 2026):</strong> server built and verified end-to-end.
                Migrating the agent tools onto it was evaluated and deliberately deferred.
            </div>

            <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">What Exists</h6>
            <p>
                <code>ClaimsIntake.McpServer</code> is a working ASP.NET MCP host built on the official
                <code>ModelContextProtocol</code> C# SDK, with dual transport — stdio (via
                <code>--stdio</code>) and HTTP. It exposes a single <code>Ping</code> tool.
            </p>
            <p>
                The server was registered with a Claude client (Claude Desktop / Claude Code) and
                <code>Ping</code> was invoked successfully across the protocol boundary. The
                <code>[Description]</code> attribute written in C# arrived at the client verbatim as the
                tool description — the same "descriptions are prompt, not documentation" principle that
                governs the in-process tools, now crossing a process boundary. Interoperability is proven;
                that was the point of the spike.
            </p>

            <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Decision — Tools Stay in Core</h6>
            <p>
                The five agent tools remain in <code>ClaimsIntake.Core</code>, registered in-process by
                <code>ClaimAssistantFactory.BuildTools</code>. Moving them was rejected for now on three
                grounds:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Single consumer.</strong> The Blazor app is the only client. The migration
                    would add a process, a deployment unit, a network hop and duplicated configuration
                    without delivering anything the in-process version does not already provide.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Duplicate embedding index.</strong> <code>SearchPolicyConditions</code> depends
                    on the in-memory vector index. The web app has to keep building its own regardless,
                    because <code>ExclusionChecker</code> calls the search directly inside the pipeline —
                    so the MCP server would build and pay for a second one.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Weakened invariant.</strong> <code>WithToolsFromAssembly()</code> is
                    convention-based auto-registration: any class marked <code>[McpServerToolType]</code>
                    is exposed automatically. That replaces the explicit five-item list which
                    <code>ClaimAssistantFactoryTests</code> pins down, and silently removes the guard
                    against a mutating tool being added unnoticed.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>Revisit when a second consumer appears</strong> — another service, a different
                agent, or an external MCP client needing the same data. Reusability is the only benefit
                that justifies the cost.
            </p>

            <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Prerequisites Before Any Real Tool Is Exposed</h6>
            <p class="mb-0">
                <code>Ping</code> is harmless. <code>GetPolicyDetail</code> is not — it returns the
                policyholder&rsquo;s name, VIN and registration plate, which is personal data under GDPR.
                Before any real tool moves onto the server: replace <code>AllowedHosts: *</code> with
                internal-only ingress, require authentication on the HTTP transport, and enforce
                authorization on <code>tools/call</code> — filtering the <code>tools/list</code> response
                alone is not a security boundary, since a client can still call a hidden tool by name.
                The tool-set test must move with the tools, scanning <code>[McpServerTool]</code>
                attributes, so the read-only guarantee does not stay behind in Core.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4 border-primary">
        <div class="card-body">
            <h4 class="card-title text-primary">Phase 3 — Multi-Agent Orchestration</h4>
            <p class="text-muted mb-3">Microsoft Agent Framework &middot; Visual verification &middot; Damage classification</p>

            <p>
                Upgrade the current single-agent pipeline to a <strong>multi-agent architecture</strong>
                using the Microsoft Agent Framework.
            </p>

            <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Photo Verification Agent</h6>
            <p>
                A dedicated agent whose sole responsibility is to verify uploaded photos against the claim report.
                It answers two questions:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Consistency check</strong> — do the photos match what the report describes?
                    If the report says "front bumper damage" but the photos show a scratched door,
                    the agent flags the discrepancy as a signal for the adjuster.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Damage classification</strong> — the model identifies which parts are damaged,
                    the extent of damage (surface scratch vs. structural deformation), and whether
                    the damage is consistent with the reported incident type.
                    Code then compares this classification against a reference table of part prices
                    and labor rates to flag invoices that fall outside the expected range.
                    The model answers "what do I see", not "how much should it cost" — the second
                    question belongs to a lookup table.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Orchestration</h6>
            <p>
                A central orchestrator decides which agents to invoke and in what order.
                For a standard claim, the investigation agent runs first; the photo agent runs
                in parallel or on demand. The orchestrator merges their findings into a single
                set of signals for the decision engine. This is more deterministic and easier
                to trace than peer-to-peer handoffs between agents.
            </p>

            <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Key Constraint</h6>
            <p class="mb-0">
                Neither agent can approve or reject a claim. They produce findings with evidence;
                the deterministic decision engine and the human adjuster remain the only decision-makers.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4 border-secondary">
        <div class="card-body">
            <h4 class="card-title text-secondary">Phase 4 — Fraud Pattern Detection</h4>
            <p class="text-muted mb-3">Vector similarity &middot; Structural queries &middot; Evaluation on labeled data</p>

            <p>
                Build a long-term memory of processed claims to detect duplicates,
                near-duplicates, and suspicious behavioral patterns at intake time.
            </p>

            <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Duplicate &amp; Near-Duplicate Detection</h6>
            <p>
                When a new claim arrives, its description and invoice details are embedded
                and compared against all stored claims using cosine similarity.
                If a highly similar claim has already been filed — same narrative, same repair shop,
                same damage pattern — the system flags it before it reaches the adjuster.
            </p>

            <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Suspicious Pattern Recognition</h6>
            <ul>
                <li>Near-duplicate incident descriptions across different contracts</li>
                <li>Clusters of suspiciously similar narratives originating from the same repair shop</li>
                <li>Semantic overlap between claims filed under different policyholders for similar vehicles</li>
            </ul>

            <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Evaluation</h6>
            <p>
                A false accusation is more expensive than a missed fraud case.
                The detection layer is evaluated on historical cases with known outcomes,
                tracking both precision and recall — not just catch rate.
                The system never blocks a claim on its own; it surfaces findings as soft signals
                in the existing decision engine for the adjuster to review.
            </p>

            <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Data Residency &amp; Retention</h6>
            <p class="mb-0">
                Claim descriptions stored as embeddings are personal data under GDPR.
                The vector store is hosted in an EU region with a defined retention period
                aligned with the insurance contract lifecycle.
                Embeddings are deleted when the retention window expires.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
